--- 15f  OBSERVATION (not part of the acceptance item): stale notice at the live edge
      Scrubbing BACKWARD then forward to the last index leaves the notice on the
      value from the earlier scrub position:
         after seek 5/5 -> 4/5 -> 3/5 -> 5/5, notice = "Nothing to remove — 0 of 93 candidates verified removable."
         expected the last event's line = "Run skipped — an earlier sweep PR is still open · pr=56 · repo=tycenjmccann/ember"
      Cause: WorkflowBoard.tsx:649 `if (!replayMode && atLiveEdge) return;` skips
      the reconstruction that owns the notice, and seekTo (:704) sets atLiveEdge
      true at the last index; the live handleEvent path deliberately does not
      write the notice (:635-640). Fresh load is correct (15e), so this only
      affects a user who scrubs back and returns. Cosmetic, pre-existing shape.

--- 15g  OBSERVATION: `finished` does not stop the SSE for a no-op close
      run-outcome-display.ts:59 documents `finished` as "pollers, intervals and
      the SSE nudge stop". Wiring check: the only consumer of outcome.finished is
      WorkflowBoard.tsx:1018 `outcome.tone === "complete" && outcome.finished`, so
      a nothing-to-remove run is never `isComplete`; wasLoadedCompleteRef stays
      false and the board takes the LIVE path, enabling the EventSource (:970).
      status pill on the finished no-op run reads: "Reconnecting..."
      What IS correctly wired for the no-op phase: the tickets poller (:492-497,
      explicit D2.9 isNoOpPhase guard), the pipeline/deploy-gate poller (:161,
      via isTerminalPhase) and the /state poller (cleared at :448 on catch-up).
      So this is the SSE only — and it is pre-existing shape shared with the
      cancelled / error / deploy-blocked / static-ci-only closes, not new in D2.9.

=== ITEM 15 RESULT: PASS ===
    One requested assertion is unsatisfiable by design: the literal reason
    slug "open-sweep-pr" is never rendered — the UI humanises it. Verified on
    the humanised wording instead, and reported as a spec/UI wording mismatch,
    not a defect.

